[38;2;124;124;124mWe have a 3D grid $Q = \{(x,y,z) \in \mathbb{Z}^3 : 0 \le x \le a, 0 \le y \le [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mb, 0 \le z \le c\}$. Pieces are placed on points in $Q$ with total $M$ pieces. [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mOperations:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m1. Remove $p$ pieces on $(x,y,z)$, place 1 on $(x-1,y,z)$[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m2. Remove $q$ pieces on $(x,y,z)$, place 1 on $(x,y-1,z)$[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m3. Remove $r$ pieces on $(x,y,z)$, place 1 on $(x,y,z-1)$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWe need the smallest $M$ such that no matter how pieces are distributed [0m
[38;2;124;124;124minitially, we can always get a piece to $(0,0,0)$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is a classic type of problem. Let me think about it using a weight/[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mmonovariant argument.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe idea: assign a weight to each position. If we assign weight $w(x,y,z)$ to [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mposition $(x,y,z)$, then for the operations to be "non-increasing" in total [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mweight, we need:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- Operation 1: $p \cdot w(x,y,z) \ge w(x-1,y,z)$, i.e., $w(x,y,z) \ge \frac{1}[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m{p} w(x-1,y,z)$[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- Operation 2: $q \cdot w(x,y,z) \ge w(x,y-1,z)$[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- Operation 3: $r \cdot w(x,y,z) \ge w(x,y,z-1)$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIf we set $w(x,y,z) = p^{-x} q^{-y} r^{-z}$, then:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- $w(x-1,y,z) = p \cdot w(x,y,z)$, so $p \cdot w(x,y,z) = w(x-1,y,z)$. This [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mmeans operation 1 preserves total weight (removes $p \cdot w(x,y,z) = [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mw(x-1,y,z)$, adds $w(x-1,y,z)$).[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- Similarly for operations 2 and 3.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo with this weight function, all operations preserve total weight. The total [0m
